# Database and Data Integrity

- Inspect the existing ORM, migration system, naming conventions and schema before changing data structures.
- Model users, jobs, companies, resumes, applications and provider records with explicit ownership, relationships, constraints and lifecycle semantics.
- Enforce user ownership in queries and mutation paths; do not rely solely on globally unique IDs.
- Add indexes based on actual query patterns and explain material trade-offs. Avoid premature indexing and unnecessary denormalization.
- Use migrations that are reviewed, reproducible and safe for existing data. Prefer expand/backfill/contract for risky production changes.
- Never reset, drop, truncate, rewrite migration history or run destructive data migrations without explicit approval and a verified backup/recovery plan.
- Keep secrets and sensitive personal information out of seed files, fixtures, logs and generated docs.
- Use transactions for invariants spanning multiple writes; define cascade behavior intentionally.
- Test migrations from a representative starting state and test rollback where supported. Do not claim reversibility if data loss makes rollback impossible.